    60	1. Introduction
    61	At ICSE04, Kitchenham et al. [23] suggested software engineer-
    62	ing researchers should adopt ‘‘Evidence-based Software Engineer-
    63	ing” (EBSE). EBSE aims to apply an evidence-based approach to
    64	software engineering research and practice. The ICSE paper was
    65	followed-up by an article in IEEE Software [5]and a paper at Met-
    66	rics05 [17].
    67	Evidence-based research and practice was developed initially in
    68	medicine because research indicated that expert opinion based
    69	medical advice was not as reliable as advice based on the accumu-
    70	lation of results from scientiﬁc experiments. Since then many do-
    71	mains have adopted this approach, e.g. Criminology, Social policy,
    72	Economics, Nursing etc. Based on Evidence-based medicine, the
    73	goal of Evidence-based Software Engineering is:
    74	‘‘To provide the means by which current best evidence from
    75	research can be integrated with practical experience and
    76	human values in the decision making process regarding the
    77	development and maintenance of software” [5].
    78	In this context, evidence is deﬁned as a synthesis of best quality
    79	scientiﬁc studies on a speciﬁc topic or research question. The main
    80	method of synthesis is a systematic literature review (SLR). In con-
    81	trast to an expert review using ad hoc literature selection, an SLR
    82	is a methodologically rigorous review of research results. The
    83	aim of an SLR is not just to aggregate all existing evidence on a re-
    84	search question; it is also intended to support the development of
    85	evidence-based guidelines for practitioners. The end point of EBSE
    86	is for practitioners to use the guidelines to provide appropriate
    87	software engineering solutions in a speciﬁc context.
    88	The purpose of this study is to review the current status of EBSE
    89	since 2004 using a tertiary study to review articles related to EBSE
    90	and, in particular, we concentrate on articles describing systematic
    91	literature reviews (SLRs). Although SLRs are not synonymous with
    92	EBSE, the aggregation of research results is an important part of the
    93	EBSE process and, furthermore, is the part of the EBSE process that
    94	can be readily observed in the scientiﬁc literature. We describe our
    95	methodology in Section 2and present our results in Section 3. In
    96	Section 4we answer our 4 major research questions. We present
    97	our conclusions in Section 5.
    98	2. Method
    99	This study has been undertaken as a systematic literature re-
   100	view based on the original guidelines as proposed by Kitchenham
   101	[22]. In this case the goal of the review is to assess systematic lit-
   102	erature reviews (which are referred to as secondary studies), so
   103	this study is categorised as a tertiary literature review. The steps
   104	in the systematic literature review method are documented below.
   105	2.1. Research questions
   106	The research questions addressed by this study are:
   107	RQ1. How much SLR activity has there been since 2004?
   108	RQ2. What research topics are being addressed?
   109	RQ3. Who is leading SLR research?
   110	RQ4. What are the limitations of current research?
   111	With respect to RQ1, it may be a concern that we started our
   112	search at the start of 2004. We recognise that the term ‘‘systematic
   113	literature review” was not in common usage in the time period
   114	during which literature reviews published in 2004 were
   115	conducted. However, there were examples both of rigours litera-
   116	ture reviews and of meta-analysis studies prior to 2004[37,41,42,10,33,29,30,13] . Furthermore, the concepts of evidence-
   117	based software engineering had been discussed by research groups
   118	in Europe for some time before 2004 as part of some (unsuccessful)
   119	European Commission Research proposals. Thus, although we
   120	would not expect papers published in 2004 to have been directly
   121	inﬂuenced by the EBSE papers [23,5] or the guidelines for system-
   122	atic reviews [22], we thought it was important to have some idea
   123	of the extent of systematic approaches to literature reviews before
   124	the guidelines were made generally available.
   125	To address RQ1, we identiﬁed the number of SLRs published per
   126	year, the journal/conferences that published them and whether or
   127	not they referenced the EBSE papers [23,5] or Guidelines paper [22].
   128	With respect to RQ2, we considered the scope of the study (i.e.
   129	whether it looked at research trends, or whether it addressed a
   130	technology-centred research question) and the software engineer-
   131	ing topic area. With respect to RQ3, we considered individual
   132	researche rs,the organisation to which researchers were afﬁliated
   133	and the country in which the organisation is situated.
   134	With respect to limitations of SLRs (RQ4) we considered a num-
   135	ber of issues:
   136	RQ4.1. Were the research topics limited?
   137	RQ4.2. Is there evidence that the use of SLRs is limited due to lack
   138	of primary studies?
   139	RQ4.3. Is the quality of SLRs appropriate, if not, is it improving?
   140	RQ4.4. Are SLRs contributing to practice by deﬁning practice
   141	guidelines?
   142	2.2. Search process
   143	The search process was a manual search of speciﬁc conference
   144	proceedings and journal papers since 2004. The selected journals
   145	and conferences are shown in Table 1 . The journals were selected
   146	because they were known to include either empirical studies or lit-
   147	erature surveys, and to have been used as sources for other system-
   148	atic literature reviews related to software engineering (e.g. [10 and
   149	36]).
   150	Each journal and conference proceedings was reviewed by one
   151	of four different researchers (i.e. Kitchenham, Brereton, Budgen
   152	and Linkman) and the papers that addressed literature surveys of
   153	any type were identiﬁed as potentially relevant. Kitchenham coor-
   154	dinated the allocation of researchers to tasks based on the avail-
   155	ability of each researcher and their ability to access the speciﬁc
   156	journals and conference proceedings. The researcher responsible
   157	for searching the speciﬁc journal or conference applied the detailed
   158	inclusion and exclusion criteria to the relevant papers (see Section
   159	2.3). Another researcher checked any papers included and ex-
   160	cluded at this stage.
