   186	2.3. Inclusion and exclusion criteria
   187	Peer-reviewed articles on the following topics, published be-
   188	tween Jan 1st 2004 and June 30th 2007, were included:
   189	/C15Systematic Literature Reviews (SLRs) i.e. literature surveys with
   190	deﬁned research questions, search process, data extraction and
   191	data presentation, whether or not the researchers referred to
   192	their study as a systematic literature review.
   193	/C15Meta-analyses (MA).
   194	Note, we included articles where the literature review was only
   195	one element of the articles as well as articles for which the litera-
   196	ture review was the main purpose of the article.
   197	Articles on the following topics were excluded
   198	/C15Informal literature surveys (no deﬁned research questions; no
   199	deﬁned search process; no deﬁned data extraction process).
   200	/C15Papers discussing the procedures used for EBSE or SLRs.
   201	/C15Duplicate reports of the same study (when several reports of a
   202	study exist in different journals the most complete version of
   203	the study was included in the review).
   204	2.4. Quality assessment
   205	Each SLR was evaluated using the York University, Centre for
   206	Reviews and Dissemination (CDR) Database of Abstracts of Re-
   207	views of Effects (DARE) criteria [3]. The criteria are based on four
   208	quality assessment (QA) questions:
   209	QA1. Are the review’s inclusion and exclusion criteria described
   210	and appropriate?
   211	QA2. Is the literature search likely to have covered all relevant
   212	studies?
   213	QA3. Did the reviewers assess the quality/validity of the included
   214	studies?
   215	QA4. Were the basic data/studies adequately described?
   216	The questions were scored as follows:
   217	/C15QA1: Y (yes), the inclusion criteria are explicitly deﬁned in the
   218	study, P (Partly), the inclusion criteria are implicit; N (no), the
   219	inclusion criteria are not deﬁned and cannot be readily inferred.
   220	/C15QA2: Y, the authors have either searched 4 or more digital
   221	libraries and included additional search strategies or identiﬁed
   222	and referenced all journals addressing the topic of interest; P,
   223	the authors have searched 3 or 4 digital libraries with no extra
   224	search strategies, or searched a deﬁned but restricted set of jour-
   225	nals and conference proceedings; N, the authors have search up
   226	to 2 digital libraries or an extremely restricted set of journals.
   227	/C15QA3: Y, the authors have explicitly deﬁned quality criteria and
   228	extracted them from each primary study; P, the research ques-
   229	tion involves quality issues that are addressed by the study; N
   230	no explicit quality assessment of individual primary studies
   231	has been attempted.
   232	/C15QA4: Y Information is presented about each study; P only sum-
   233	mary information about primary studies is presented; N the
   234	results of the individual primary studies are not speciﬁed.The scoring procedure was Y = 1, P = 0.5, N = 0, or Unknown (i.e.
   235	the information is not speciﬁed). Kitchenham coordinated the
   236	quality evaluation extraction process. Kitchenham assessed every
   237	paper, and allocated 4 papers to each of the other authors of this
   238	study to assess independently. When there was a disagreement,
   239	we discussed the issues until we reached agreement. When a ques-
   240	tion was scored as unknown we e-mailed the authors of the paper
   241	and asked them to provide the relevant information and the ques-
   242	tion re-scored appropriately.
   243	2.5. Data collection
   244	The data extracted from each study were:
   245	/C15The source (journal or conference) and full reference.
   246	/C15Classiﬁcation of the study Type (SLR, Meta-Analysis MA); Scope
   247	(Research trends or speciﬁc technology evaluation question).
   248	/C15Main topic area.
   249	/C15The author(s) and their institution and the country where it is
   250	situated.
   251	/C15Summary of the study including the main research questions
   252	and the answers.
   253	/C15Research question/issue.
   254	/C15Quality evaluation.
   255	/C15Whether the study referenced the EBSE papers [23,5] or the SLR
   256	Guidelines [22].
   257	/C15Whether thestudy proposed practitioner-based guidelines.
   258	/C15How many primary studies were used in the SLR.
