   402	was not signiﬁcant ( F= 0.37, p= 0.55). Thus, it appears that the
   403	quality of SLRs is improving but the improvement cannot be attrib-
   404	uted to the guidelines.
   405	4. Discussion
   406	In this section, we discuss the answers to our research
   407	questions.
   408	4.1. How much EBSE Activity has there been since 2004?
   409	Overall, we identiﬁed 20 relevant studies in the sources that we
   410	searched, as shown in Table 2 . 19 studies were classiﬁed as SLRs
   411	and one study was classiﬁed as a meta-analysis [8]. Twelve studies
   412	addressed technology evaluation issues and 8 addressed research
   413	trends. We found that 8 studies referenced Kitchenham’s guide-lines [22] and two referenced the EBSE paper [5]. Thus, half the
   414	studies directly positioned themselves as related to Evidence-
   415	based Software Engineering.
   416	With respect to where SLRs are published, IEEE Software and
   417	IEEE TSE each published 4 studies, JSS published 3 and IST pub-
   418	lished 2. Thus, it appeared that IST’s attempts to encourage the
   419	publication of SLRs, was unsuccessful [6]. However, a further check
   420	of IST publications (on September 17th 2008 using the search
   421	string systematic AND review ) found seven more SLRs, whereas sim-
   422	ilar searches of TSE and JSS found no new SLRs.
   423	Initially, we were surprised that ACM Computer Surveys did not
   424	include any relevant software engineering studies, although the
   425	journal published a systematic literature review on the topic of
   426	education [25]. An automated search of ACM Computer Surveys
   427	using the ACM digital library on September 20th 2008, found no
   428	software-related surveys that used the systematic review method-
   429	ology. However, the apparent lack of software SLRs in ACM Com-
   430	puter Surveys may be because, with a maximum of four issues
   431	per year, the journal is likely to have a signiﬁcant publication lag.
   432	4.2. What research topics are being addressed?
   433	With respect to the topic of the articles, eight were related to re-
   434	search trends rather than speciﬁc research questions. In terms of
   435	the software engineering topic area addressed by the SLRs:
   436	/C157 related to software cost estimation (one of those covered
   437	research trends), in addition, the four studies that included evi-
   438	dence-based guidelines all related to cost estimation.
   439	/C153 articles related to software engineering experiments (all inves-
   440	tigated research trends).
   441	/C153 articles related to test methods.
