   647	software technology evaluation.
   648	/C15A single researcher selected the candidate studies, although the
   649	studies included and excluded were checked by another
   650	researcher.
   651	/C15A single researcher extracted the data and another researcher
   652	checked the data extraction, as suggested by Brereton et al. [2].
   653	The ﬁrst point above implies that we may have missed some
   654	relevant studies, and thus underestimate the extent of EBSE-re-
   655	lated research. In particular, we will have missed articles published
   656	in national journals and conferences. We will also have missed
   657	articles in conferences aimed at speciﬁc software engineering top-
   658	ics which are more likely to have addressed research questions
   659	rather than research trends. Thus, our results must be qualiﬁed
   660	as applying only to systematic literature reviews published in the
   661	major international software engineering journals, and the major
   662	general and empirical software engineering conferences.
   663	With respect to the second point, given our interest in system-
   664	atic literature reviews, we are likely to have erred on the side ofincluding studies that were not very systematic, rather than omit-
   665	ting any relevant studies. For example, the literature review in the
   666	primary study, that was assigned the lowest quality score [38], was
   667	only a minor part of the article.
   668	The third point means that some of the data we collected may
   669	be erroneous. A detailed review of one of our own systematic liter-
   670	ature reviews has suggested that the extractor/checker mode of
   671	working can lead to data extraction and aggregation problems
   672	when there are a large number of primary studies or the data is
   673	complex [39]. However, in this tertiary study, there were relatively
   674	few primary studies and the data extracted from the selected arti-
   675	cles were relatively objective, so we do not expect many data
   676	extraction errors. The quality assessment criteria proved the most
   677	difﬁcult data to extract because the DARE criteria are somewhat
   678	subjective. However quality criteria were evaluated independently
   679	by two researchers, hopefully reducing the likelihood of erroneous
   680	results.
   681	5. Conclusions
   682	Although 10 of the SLR studies in this review cited one of the
   683	EBSE papers [5]or the SLR Guidelines [22], the number of SLRs
   684	has remained extremely stable in the 3.5 years included in this
   685	study. Furthermore, Table A2 (see Appendix 1 ) also makes it clear
   686	that many researchers still prefer to undertake informal literature
   687	surveys. However, we have found that the quality of SLRs is improv-
   688	ing, suggesting that researchers who are interested in the EBSE ap-
   689	proach are becoming more competent in the SLR methodology.
   690	The spread of topics covered by current SLRs is fairly limited.
   691	Furthermore main stream software engineering topics are not well
   692	represented. However, even if these areas are unsuitable for SLRs
   693	aimed at empirical assessments of software technology, we believe
